    "message": {
      "content": "# Distributed Tensor and Expert Parallel Inference: A Technical Note\n\n## 1. Overview and Motivation\n\nModern large models exceed the memory and compute capacity of a single accelerator. Two complementary parallelism strategies address this during inference:\n\n- **Tensor Parallelism (TP)**: Splits individual weight matrices (and thus individual layers' compute) across devices. Every device holds a shard of every layer and participates in every forward pass.\n- **Expert Parallelism (EP)**: Splits the *experts* of a Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) layer across devices. Each device holds a subset of experts in full; tokens are routed to whichever device owns the relevant expert.\n\nTP is the workhorse for dense models and for the attention/dense-MLP portions of MoE models. EP is essential for MoE models where the total expert parameter count is enormous but per-token activation is sparse. In production systems (e.g., DeepSeek, Mixtral-class deployments), TP and EP are typically combined: TP for attention and shared layers, EP for the MoE feed-forward blocks.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Tensor Parallel Inference\n\n### 2.1 Sharding scheme\n\nConsider a transformer layer with:\n\n- Attention projection: $Y = XW_Q, XW_K, XW_V$, output $O = A W_O$\n- MLP: $H = \\text{act}(X W_1)$, $Y = H W_2$\n\nThe standard Megatron-style layout:\n\n- **Column-parallel**: $W_Q, W_K, W_V, W_1$ are split along the *output* dimension. Each device computes its slice of the hidden features: $H_i = \\text{act}(X W_1^{(i)})$.\n- **Row-parallel**: $W_O, W_2$ are split along the *input* dimension. Each device computes a partial output using its slice of the activations:",
      "reasoning": "",
      "role": "assistant"
    },
